#Patches Dc Blocker

A DC blocker removes a constant or slowly-drifting offset from a signal
while leaving the audible band essentially untouched.  Such offsets
creep in after waveshaping, bias injection, or any nonlinearity that is
not perfectly symmetric; left in place they waste headroom and can thump
through downstream filters.

The kernel is a one-pole highpass with its corner placed around 5 Hz —
below hearing, above DC:

```text
y[n] = x[n] - x[n-1] + R * y[n-1]
R = 1 - 2*pi*fc / sample_rate
```

The pole radius `R` sits just inside the unit circle, so the feedback
term `R * y[n-1]` decays slowly.  During silence that decaying tail is
exactly the kind of value that drifts subnormal, so each output is
passed through #Patches Denormal's `flush_denormal` before it is stored
as state.

    #[derive(Clone)]
    pub struct DcBlocker {
        x_prev: f32,
        y_prev: f32,
        r: f32,
    }

    impl DcBlocker {
        pub fn new(sample_rate: f32) -> Self {
            Self {
                x_prev: 0.0,
                y_prev: 0.0,
                r: 1.0 - TAU * 5.0 / sample_rate,
            }
        }

        #[inline]
        pub fn process(&mut self, x: f32) -> f32 {
            let y = flush_denormal(x - self.x_prev + self.r * self.y_prev);
            self.x_prev = x;
            self.y_prev = y;
            y
        }

        pub fn reset(&mut self) {
            self.x_prev = 0.0;
            self.y_prev = 0.0;
        }
    }

Fed a constant input, the output settles toward zero — the offset is
removed.  After a #reset the filter behaves as freshly constructed:
silence in, silence out.

~example
    { let mut dc = DcBlocker::new(44100.0); for _ in 0..44100 { dc.process(1.0); } dc.process(1.0).abs() < 0.01 }
    { let mut dc = DcBlocker::new(44100.0); dc.process(1.0); dc.process(1.0); dc.reset(); dc.process(0.0) == 0.0 }

##Stability and silence

The filter is stable: a bounded input yields a bounded output, and a
long run of any constant input never escapes to a non-finite value.

~property bounded-output
    proptest! {
        fn finite_under_constant_drive(x in -1.0f32..1.0) {
            let mut dc = DcBlocker::new(44100.0);
            let mut out = 0.0f32;
            for _ in 0..2000 {
                out = dc.process(x);
            }
            prop_assert!(out.is_finite());
        }
    }

Prolonged silence after an impulse must not produce subnormal stalls —
the #flush_denormal guard guarantees every stored output is zero or
normal.

~property silence-has-no-denormals
    proptest! {
        fn silence_is_clean(seed in -1.0f32..1.0) {
            let mut dc = DcBlocker::new(44100.0);
            dc.process(seed);
            let mut out = 0.0f32;
            for _ in 0..10000 {
                out = dc.process(0.0);
            }
            prop_assert!(out == 0.0 || out.is_normal());
        }
    }

---

#Tests

##removes a constant offset
    { let mut dc = DcBlocker::new(44100.0); for _ in 0..44100 { dc.process(1.0); } dc.process(1.0).abs() < 0.01 }

##passes an alternating signal
    { let mut dc = DcBlocker::new(44100.0); for _ in 0..4410 { dc.process(0.5); dc.process(-0.5); } dc.process(0.5).abs() > 0.1 }

##reset clears state
    { let mut dc = DcBlocker::new(44100.0); dc.process(1.0); dc.process(1.0); dc.reset(); dc.process(0.0) == 0.0 }

